# .env.example — copy this file to .env and fill in the real values.
#
#   copy .env.example .env      (Windows)
#   cp .env.example .env        (Linux/macOS)
#
# THIS file IS committed. The .env you create from it is NOT: it is listed in
# .gitignore, and the commit hook refuses a staged key.
#
# A machine-level environment variable also works and takes priority over
# anything written here. Setting one needs administrator rights, which a
# managed machine may not give - so .env is the supported path. On a hosting
# platform the values go into the platform's own settings; the code reads
# os.environ either way, so nothing in the code changes.
#
# ONE BLOCK PER VARIABLE. Say what it is, who owns it, and what the value
# means - the next person to open this file is renewing a key under time
# pressure and should not have to go and ask.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EXAMPLE — delete this block and write your own.
#
# What it is : access key for the Acme completions API.
# Who owns it: <a NAMED person, not a team - they renew it>
# Where from : the Acme console, Settings -> API keys.
#
# ACME_API_KEY=replace-me
#
# The expiry date lives here and nowhere else - a date written twice is a
# date that will disagree with itself. Format YYYY-MM-DD.
#
# ACME_API_KEY_EXPIRES=2027-03-14
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
